# Why Unite, not the alternatives

Three honest comparisons.

---

# vs. "we'll build it ourselves"

The brokerage's IT shop says "give us six months and we'll do it." They won't.

- **Unite is .NET 10 + Blazor + EF Core** — the stack their team already runs
- **Unite has 70+ entities and 200+ handlers** — six months of in-house work, minimum
- **Unite has AI integrations** they'd never build (Gemini, OCR, durable orchestration)
- **Unite has six Azure Function bridges** to AH, FinOps, Workday, Mulesoft — fully wired
- **In-house effort burns 2-3 FTEs for a year** — Unite ships in weeks

*Talk track:* "Your team is great. They're not building a six-person-year-equivalent product on the side."

---

# vs. a generic ERP (NetSuite, D365 Finance)

Generic ERPs don't speak insurance.

- **No native concept of "commission statement"** — every carrier integration is custom
- **No native concept of "split intake"** — producer compensation is configured, not modeled
- **No native concept of "research case"** — unmatched cash sits in clearing accounts
- **Customization cost: $500k-$2M** — and a multi-year project
- **Unite is purpose-built for brokerage finance** — these concepts are first-class

*Talk track:* "NetSuite is a great accounting platform. It's not an insurance platform."

---

# vs. niche brokerage software (BenefitMall, AgencyBloc, etc.)

Niche brokerage software handles agency management. It doesn't handle the GL.

- **Niche tools own producer + policy data** — fine
- **Niche tools don't integrate to the GL** — finance still rekeys
- **Niche tools have no AI on the import side** — manual templates forever
- **Unite is the missing finance layer** — it sits next to the niche tools, not against them
- **Unite is also a credible upgrade path** when the niche tool's accounting module gives out

*Talk track:* "Keep your AMS. Add Unite where the GL lives. We don't compete with your AMS — we complete the picture."

